3.35.164.290 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"In computing, both **processes** and **threads** are fundamental units of execution, but they differ significantly in how they operate and share resources. Understanding the difference is crucial for designing efficient systems like **web servers**.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 **Process**\nA **process** is an independent program in execution. It has its own:\n- Memory space\n- File descriptors\n- Environment variables\n- Execution stack\n\nProcesses are **isolated** from each other. Communication between processes (Inter-Process Communication, or IPC) is more complex and slower (e.g., using pipes, sockets, or shared memory).\n\n### 🔹 **Thread**\nA **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process. Multiple threads can exist within the same process and **share**:\n- Memory space\n- Open files\n- Global variables\n- Signal handlers\n\nEach thread has its own:\n- Stack\n- Program counter\n- Register set\n\nThreads are **lightweight** and can communicate more easily and quickly than processes.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 Key Differences\n\n| Feature              | Process                          | Thread                              |\n|----------------------|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n"}
