3.46.715.045 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"## Trade-offs of Adding a Message Queue Between Two Services\n\n### What It Buys You\n\n- **Decoupling.** The producer and consumer no longer need to know about each other's network location, availability, or implementation. They interact only with the queue.\n- **Resilience & Fault Isolation.** If the consumer goes down, messages accumulate in the queue and are processed once it recovers. The producer never fails because a downstream service is unavailable.\n- **Elasticity & Load Leveling.** The queue absorbs traffic spikes. Producers can publish at full speed while the consumer processes at its own sustainable pace, preventing cascading overload.\n- **Asynchrony.** The producer doesn't block waiting for a response, improving latency from the user's perspective and freeing up resources.\n- **Scalability.** Multiple consumer instances can read from the same queue (competing consumers pattern), making it easy to scale processing horizontally.\n- **Ordering Guarantees (when needed).** Many queues provide per-partition or per-queue ordering, useful for event sourcing and audit trails.\n- **Replayability.** Messages persist, so you can replay, reprocess, or debug them after the fact.\n\n### What It Costs You\n\n- **Operational Complexity"}
